# {{ app_name }}

Spring Boot {{ versions.spring_boot }} service in Kotlin {{ versions.kotlin }} on Java {{ versions.java }}, generated by Prism's `spring-backend` pack for {{ project_name }}. Port {{ port }}.

This is a starting point, not a finished backend. It holds one compiling slice with its tests:

- `POST /api/dev-identity/token` signs a development token. It exists only under the `local` Spring profile.
- `GET /api/dev-identity/jwks` publishes the public key that verifies those tokens, as a JWKS document, so another local service can validate them with no shared secret. Same profile and loopback rules.
- `GET /api/me` returns the profile of the signed-in user. It needs a bearer JWT.
- `GET /actuator/health` reports the app and its database.

The slice has no business features. Build yours beside it, following its structure.

## Requirements

- JDK {{ versions.java }}
- Docker, for the integration tests (Testcontainers) and the local development database

No credentials of any kind are needed to build, test or start the app.

## Run it locally

From the repository root, start the workspace's PostgreSQL, then run the app with the `local` profile:

```bash
task db-up
task {{ app_id }}:dev
```

Without Task: `docker compose up -d db`, then `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=local ./gradlew bootRun` in `{{ app_path }}/` (PowerShell: `$env:SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE = "local"`).

Sign in and read the profile:

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:{{ port }}/api/dev-identity/token \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "ada@example.test", "displayName": "Ada"}'
# {"accessToken":"<token>","tokenType":"Bearer","expiresIn":900}

curl -s http://localhost:{{ port }}/api/me -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>"
```

The same email always signs in as the same user. Tokens last 15 minutes and stop working when the app restarts, because the signing key lives only in memory.

## The dev identity is local development sign-in

It is not authentication. The `local` profile signs tokens for anyone on this machine:

- the token route and the JWKS route answer requests from the loopback interface only, and answer 404 under every other profile;
- the JWKS carries the public key only; the signing key stays in memory;
- the signing key is generated in memory at startup and is never written to disk, and no JWT secret exists in the template;
- startup fails when the `local` profile is active together with a configured identity provider;
- the generated `docker-compose.yml` sets no profile, so a container never runs the dev identity.

Never enable the `local` profile on a shared or deployed environment. Before the app is exposed, replace the dev identity with your identity provider; the `security-auth` skill (`.claude/skills/security-auth/` or `.agents/skills/security-auth/`) walks through it.

## Tests

```bash
./gradlew test      # or: task {{ app_id }}:test
```

- Integration tests (`DefaultProfileIntegrationTest`, `LocalProfileIntegrationTest`, `OpenApiContractTest`) boot the whole app against a PostgreSQL started by Testcontainers (`postgres:{{ versions.postgres }}`). Docker must be running. They never touch the development database.
- `DevIdentityStartupGuardTest` proves that the `local` profile with a configured issuer refuses to start.
- Unit tests (`UserServiceTest`, `DevIdentityTokenServiceTest`, `LoopbackRequestPolicyTest`) need no Docker.

The app's CI workflow, `.github/workflows/{{ app_id }}.yml`, runs `./gradlew build` (compile, every test, the jar) on a clean runner with no secrets.

## Layout

```
src/main/kotlin/{{ app_package_path }}/
  bootstrap/    security, dev identity wiring, properties
  modules/      users/ (GET /api/me), devidentity/ (the local token and JWKS routes)
  shared/       error model, exception handler, audit base class
src/main/resources/db/migration/   Flyway migrations (V1__users.sql)
```

Tables live in the `{{ app_id | replace('-', '_') }}` schema of the database (`DATABASE_SCHEMA`), so several backends can share the workspace database. See `docs/guide.md` for conventions and `AGENTS.md` for the rules agents follow.
